    <!-- 0 WELCOME -->
    <section class="screen active" data-teach="intro">
      <span class="tag">Mini-Lesson</span>
      <h1>Theme B: Religion &amp; Life</h1>
      <p class="big">This mini-lesson works through the AQA GCSE theme <b>Religion and Life</b>: the <b>origins of the universe and of life</b>, <b>evolution</b>, the <b>value of the world and stewardship</b>, the <b>use and abuse of the environment and of animals</b>, and the debates around <b>abortion</b>, <b>euthanasia</b> and <b>life after death</b>.</p>
      <figure>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 560 150" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="Religious and non-religious views on life questions">
          <rect x="18" y="45" width="150" height="62" rx="14" fill="#d9ebff" stroke="#2c2840" stroke-width="3"/>
          <text x="93" y="72" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Fredoka" font-size="14" font-weight="600" fill="#0b5bbf">Science &amp;</text>
          <text x="93" y="92" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Fredoka" font-size="13" fill="#0b5bbf">religion</text>
          <rect x="392" y="45" width="150" height="62" rx="14" fill="#ffe9c7" stroke="#2c2840" stroke-width="3"/>
          <text x="467" y="72" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Fredoka" font-size="14" font-weight="600" fill="#9a5a00">Ethical</text>
          <text x="467" y="92" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Fredoka" font-size="13" fill="#9a5a00">questions</text>
          <line x1="168" y1="76" x2="382" y2="76" stroke="#34c777" stroke-width="5"/>
          <polygon points="382,76 366,68 366,84" fill="#34c777"/>
          <text x="275" y="62" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Fredoka" font-size="13" font-weight="600" fill="#137a45">shape views on</text>
          <text x="275" y="100" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Nunito" font-size="12.5" fill="#6b6580">how we should live and value life</text>
        </svg>
      </figure>
      <p>Work through each screen, answer the <b>questions</b> as you go and collect ⭐ stars. A <b>range</b> of religious (Christian, Muslim) and non-religious (Humanist) views is presented fairly. Press <b>Start</b> when you're ready.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- 1 ORIGINS OF THE UNIVERSE -->
    <section class="screen" data-teach="origins-universe">
      <span class="tag lo">Origins of the universe</span>
      <h2>The Big Bang and creation</h2>
      <p>Scientists explain the origin of the universe with the <span class="hl k">Big Bang theory</span>: around 13.8 billion years ago all matter and energy expanded from a single point, and the universe has been expanding ever since. Religious believers give a further explanation: God is the <b>Creator</b>.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Many <b>Christians</b> read <b>Genesis 1</b> as teaching that God created the universe, and see the Big Bang as <b>how</b> God did it — science and faith answering different questions.</li>
        <li>Many <b>Muslims</b> believe <b>Allah</b> created the universe and point to Qur'anic verses describing the heavens and earth being "joined together" then parted, which some see as fitting the Big Bang.</li>
        <li>Some Christians and Muslims are <b>creationists</b> who read the creation accounts more literally and reject parts of the scientific account.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="note"><b>Scripture:</b> "In the beginning God created the heavens and the earth." (<i>Genesis 1:1</i>) Science and religion are not necessarily in conflict — many believers accept both.</div>
    </section>

    <!-- 2 ORIGINS OF LIFE & EVOLUTION -->
    <section class="screen" data-teach="evolution">
      <span class="tag lo">Origins of life &amp; evolution</span>
      <h2>Evolution and creation</h2>
      <p>The <span class="hl c">theory of evolution</span>, developed by <b>Charles Darwin</b>, explains that species change over time through <b>natural selection</b>: those best suited to their environment are more likely to survive and pass on their features. This is the scientific account of the origins of life's diversity.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Many <b>Christians</b> and <b>Muslims</b> accept evolution as the <b>method God used</b> to create living things — God set the process going.</li>
        <li>Some believers reject evolution and hold to <b>special creation</b> — that God created living things in their present forms.</li>
        <li>For many, humans still have a <b>special status</b> as made "in the image of God" (Christian) or given a soul by Allah (Muslim), whatever the biological process.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="note"><b>Key point:</b> a common exam mistake is to say science and religion must contradict each other. Many religious believers accept both — do not present them as necessarily opposed.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- 3 Q: origins MCQ -->
    <section class="screen" data-test="q-origins">
      <span class="tag">Quick check</span>
      <h2>Science and religion on origins</h2>
      <div class="activity">
        <div class="qline"><span class="qnum">?</span><span>Which statement best reflects how <b>many</b> religious believers view the Big Bang and evolution?</span></div>
        <div class="opts" data-q="q1" data-why="Many Christians and Muslims accept the scientific account and see the Big Bang and evolution as HOW God created — science and religion answering different questions rather than necessarily conflicting.">
          <button class="opt" data-correct="0">Every religious believer rejects all science <span class="mk">❌</span></button>
          <button class="opt" data-correct="1">Many accept them as the way God created the universe and life <span class="mk">✅</span></button>
          <button class="opt" data-correct="0">Science has proved that God does not exist <span class="mk">❌</span></button>
          <button class="opt" data-correct="0">Religion and science must always contradict each other <span class="mk">❌</span></button>
        </div>
        <div class="feedback" id="fb-q1"></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 4 VALUE OF THE WORLD & STEWARDSHIP -->
    <section class="screen" data-teach="stewardship">
      <span class="tag lo">Value of the world &amp; stewardship</span>
      <h2>Dominion and stewardship</h2>
      <p>Many religions teach that the world is valuable because it is created by God, and that humans have a <b>duty to care for it</b>. Two key ideas come from Genesis:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><span class="hl g">Dominion</span> — humans are given authority over the earth (<i>Genesis 1:28</i>), but this is widely understood as <b>responsible</b> rule, not the right to exploit.</li>
        <li><span class="hl c">Stewardship</span> — humans are trustees who look after creation for God and future generations (<i>Genesis 2:15</i>: "to work it and take care of it").</li>
      </ul>
      <p>In Islam, humans are <b>khalifah</b> (stewards/vicegerents) accountable to Allah for how they treat the natural world.</p>
      <div class="note"><b>Scripture:</b> God placed the human "in the Garden of Eden to work it and take care of it." (<i>Genesis 2:15</i>) — the basis of Christian stewardship.</div>
    </section>

    <!-- 5 USE & ABUSE OF ENVIRONMENT & ANIMALS -->
    <section class="screen" data-teach="environment-animals">
      <span class="tag lo">Use &amp; abuse of the environment and animals</span>
      <h2>Resources, pollution and animals</h2>
      <p>Human activity can <b>use</b> or <b>abuse</b> creation. Using up natural resources, causing <span class="hl t">pollution</span> and damaging the environment are seen by many believers as failing in stewardship. Reducing waste, recycling and protecting the planet are seen as religious duties.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Natural resources</b> — believers may argue we should use resources wisely and sustainably, not greedily.</li>
        <li><b>Animals for food</b> — many Christians and Muslims accept eating meat; some Christians choose <b>vegetarianism</b>. In Islam meat should be <b>halal</b> and slaughter should avoid cruelty.</li>
        <li><b>Animal experimentation</b> — some accept it where it reduces human suffering; others oppose cruelty. Most traditions forbid causing needless suffering to animals.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="note"><b>Balance:</b> most religious views permit the <b>humane</b> use of animals but reject <b>cruelty</b>. Positions on experimentation and vegetarianism vary within and between religions.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- 6 Q: match key term to meaning (match game) -->
